const appscPrelimsAndGroup2Lessons = {
  'g2-m-ap-dynasties': [
    ['Satavahanas and the Krishna valley', 'The Satavahanas built a kingdom across much of the Deccan in the centuries around the beginning of the Common Era. Gautamiputra Satakarni restored their power against the Western Kshatrapas. Their coins and inscriptions give evidence of rulers, regional exchange and donations to religious institutions.'],
    ['Ikshvakus and the growth of Buddhist centres', 'The Ikshvakus followed the Satavahanas in parts of the lower Krishna valley in the third century CE. At Nagarjunakonda, inscriptions and excavated monasteries, stupas and other buildings reveal an active religious centre. Amaravati and Nagarjunakonda both preserve Buddhist sculpture, but each has a distinct archaeological history.'],
    ['Eastern Chalukyas and Kakatiyas', 'The Eastern Chalukyas ruled from Vengi from the early medieval period and became closely connected to the Cholas through dynastic alliances. Telugu literary culture developed under court patronage. The Kakatiyas ruled from Warangal, supported irrigation tanks and drew revenue from agriculture and trade. Their Ramappa temple offers an example of Kakatiya stone sculpture and architecture.'],
    ['Vijayanagara and regional culture', 'Vijayanagara later connected Telugu-speaking regions with a wider south Indian empire. At Lepakshi, the Virabhadra temple and its paintings illustrate the role of patronage in workshop traditions and religious life. For an exam timeline, match each polity to its region and one source of evidence, such as a coin, inscription or monument.']
  ],
  'g2-m-ap-culture': [
    ['The development of Telugu literature', 'Nannaya began the Telugu rendering of the Mahabharata under Eastern Chalukya patronage. Tikkana and Errana continued the project across later periods. Krishnadevaraya’s Amuktamalyada illustrates Telugu literary production at the Vijayanagara court.'],
    ['Performance, sculpture and architecture', 'Kuchipudi developed as a dance-drama tradition and uses movement, expression and music to advance a narrative. The limestone reliefs of Amaravati belong to a Buddhist sculptural tradition, while Lepakshi is associated with Vijayanagara temple architecture and painting. Identify the dynasty, medium and subject before comparing two works.'],
    ['Reform through institutions and literature', 'Kandukuri Veeresalingam used education, writing and organized action to promote women’s education and widow remarriage. Gurajada Apparao’s play Kanyasulkam used everyday Telugu and satire to criticize social practices around marriage. Veeresalingam illustrates institution-building, while Gurajada shows how theatre can change public debate.']
  ],
  'g2-m-ap-statehood': [
    ['The Andhra movement and the 1953 state', 'Telugu-speaking leaders in the composite Madras Presidency organized for a separate administrative unit. Potti Sriramulu’s death after a fast in 1952 intensified the movement. Andhra State was formed on 1 October 1953, with Kurnool as its capital.'],
    ['The 1956 merger', 'The 1956 reorganization combined Andhra State with the Telangana region of the former Hyderabad State to create Andhra Pradesh on 1 November. Hyderabad became the capital of the enlarged state. The Gentlemen’s Agreement set out safeguards discussed during the merger, especially around regional administration, jobs and expenditure.'],
    ['Bifurcation and its practical consequences', 'The Andhra Pradesh Reorganisation Act, 2014 provided for Telangana to become a separate state from 2 June 2014. It dealt with subjects including the division of assets and liabilities, allocation of employees, water management and transitional institutions. For later developments, consult the Act, amendments, official budget documents and dated government orders.']
  ],
  'g2-m-constitutional-basics': [
    ['From colonial laws to the Constituent Assembly', 'The Government of India Act, 1935 introduced provincial autonomy and provided administrative arrangements that influenced later constitutional design. The Constituent Assembly first met in December 1946. Its committees developed proposals and the Drafting Committee, chaired by B. R. Ambedkar, converted the proposals into a constitutional draft.'],
    ['Adoption, commencement and the Preamble', 'The Assembly adopted the Constitution on 26 November 1949 and most provisions came into force on 26 January 1950. The Preamble describes the character of the republic and its commitments to justice, liberty, equality and fraternity. The Supreme Court’s basic-structure doctrine establishes that Parliament’s power to amend has constitutional limits.'],
    ['Rights, directives and duties', 'Part III contains judicially enforceable Fundamental Rights, including equality and protections for individual freedoms. Part IV contains Directive Principles that guide policy and legislation. Part IVA lists Fundamental Duties for citizens, such as protecting the natural environment. In a practice answer, explain the distinct legal role of each part before using an example.']
  ],
  'g2-m-federal': [
    ['Division of powers and parliamentary government', 'The Seventh Schedule organizes legislative subjects across the Union, State and Concurrent Lists. Parliament and state legislatures can both make law on Concurrent List matters, subject to constitutional rules on conflicts. In parliamentary government the Union Council of Ministers is collectively responsible to the Lok Sabha and a state Council of Ministers to its Legislative Assembly.'],
    ['Courts and constitutional remedies', 'The Supreme Court heads an integrated judiciary and hears constitutional disputes and appeals under the conditions prescribed by law. High Courts hear cases at the state level and can issue writs under Article 226. Article 32 gives a route to the Supreme Court for enforcement of Fundamental Rights.'],
    ['Independent constitutional offices', 'The Election Commission supervises specified national and state elections under Article 324. The Comptroller and Auditor General audits public finances under its constitutional framework and applicable law. The Finance Commission recommends aspects of tax distribution and grants, while public service commissions perform recruitment and advisory functions.']
  ],
  'g2-m-governance': [
    ['Local representation and administration', 'The 73rd and 74th Amendments established constitutional frameworks for Panchayats and Municipalities. A Gram Sabha is a body of persons on the village electoral rolls, while a Gram Panchayat is an elected institution. State law determines details of powers and administration, and State Election Commissions supervise local elections.'],
    ['Amendment and judicial review', 'Article 368 provides a special amendment procedure. Some changes also require ratification by at least half of the states. Courts can examine whether legislation complies with the Constitution, while the basic-structure doctrine limits constitutional amendments themselves.'],
    ['Citizen rights in daily administration', 'A person denied a public benefit can ask for the eligibility rules, obtain a written reason and use the appropriate grievance or appeal channel. The Right to Information Act provides a route to obtain information from public authorities, subject to its rules and exemptions. Use the example to distinguish transparency, which makes information visible, from accountability, which requires an explanation and a remedy.']
  ],
  'g2-m-economy': [
    ['National income, sectors and development', 'GDP measures the value of production within a country during a period, while gross value added measures output less intermediate consumption at the producer or sector level. Comparing real GDP uses constant prices to separate the change in output from price movements. Development also involves health, education and participation, so output growth alone provides an incomplete picture.'],
    ['Poverty, employment and inflation', 'Poverty can be examined through household spending or through multiple deprivations such as nutrition and schooling. The unemployment rate refers to the labour force, which excludes people outside work and job search or availability under the measure used. A broad increase in prices erodes purchasing power, especially when wages adjust slowly.'],
    ['The budget and taxation', 'A government budget balances priorities through receipts, spending and borrowing. Revenue expenditure finances recurring services and transfers, while capital expenditure can create assets such as roads or schools. Direct taxes are imposed on income or similar bases, while GST is an indirect tax on supplies. The fiscal deficit indicates the borrowing requirement after excluding borrowing from receipts.']
  ],
  'g2-m-sectors': [
    ['Agriculture, industry and services', 'Agriculture supplies food and industrial inputs and supports many rural livelihoods. Industry transforms inputs such as crops or minerals into goods, while services include transport, retail, finance, education and health. A cold-storage network can connect all three by reducing crop losses and helping processing firms reach larger markets.'],
    ['Banking and investment', 'Banks connect savers and borrowers, extend credit and help households and firms make payments. Credit can fund machinery or working capital, and debt service depends on the borrower’s future cash flow. Financial inclusion combines access with meaningful and safe use of banking and payment services.'],
    ['Infrastructure and trade', 'Reliable roads, power, ports and logistics lower the time and cost of production and exchange. Exports bring foreign exchange and import demand can reflect reliance on fuel, technology or intermediate inputs. Evaluate infrastructure by its effect on market access, environmental costs and who benefits, rather than by construction spending alone.']
  ],
  'g2-m-ap-economy': [
    ['Reading state income and sector performance', 'Gross State Domestic Product estimates production within state boundaries. Constant-price GSDP is useful for comparing output over time, while current-price GSDP is used for ratios such as debt to GSDP. Compare each sector’s contribution to output with its share of employment to see where productivity and livelihood patterns differ.'],
    ['Agriculture, irrigation and industry', 'The Krishna and Godavari deltas support irrigated paddy farming, while drier parts of Rayalaseema have different crop and water needs. Canals, tanks and groundwater all contribute to irrigation, with distinct costs and risks. Food processing, coastal logistics and manufacturing can add value when linked to producers, transport and reliable electricity.'],
    ['State budget and social programs', 'State receipts include the state’s own revenue, Union tax devolution and grants. Spending on education, health and social protection should be evaluated by reach and outcomes alongside budget allocations. Use a recent state budget and socioeconomic survey with the reference year written beside each figure.']
  ],
  'g2-m-science': [
    ['Everyday science and the innovation system', 'A pressure cooker raises the boiling temperature of water by increasing pressure, while a refrigerator transfers heat from its cooler interior to warmer surroundings using work. Basic research develops knowledge and applied research adapts it to a practical problem. Testing, standards and adoption determine whether the resulting technology works outside the laboratory.'],
    ['Information technology, space and public services', 'Databases, networks and identity checks let public portals process applications and show their status. Secure access and offline options affect who can use those services. Satellites support communications, navigation and remote sensing, and remote sensing helps monitor crop stress, floods and coastlines. In defence, sensors and communication systems illustrate applications of shared engineering principles.'],
    ['Biotechnology and health technologies', 'Biotechnology applies living systems or their components to useful products and measurements. Diagnostic tests help identify a condition, while vaccines train immune responses to recognize particular targets. To evaluate a proposal, ask about evidence, the intended users, cost, equitable access and safeguards for personal information.']
  ],
  'g2-m-environment': [
    ['Energy and its environmental costs', 'Fossil fuels supply controllable energy while producing greenhouse gases and air pollutants when burned. Wind and solar supply low-emission electricity during operation, with output that varies over time. Storage, grid links, flexible demand and other generation help match electricity supply to demand.'],
    ['Environmental degradation and climate response', 'Contaminated water, habitat loss and soil erosion can damage both ecosystems and livelihoods. Climate mitigation reduces greenhouse gas emissions or increases removals, while adaptation reduces harm from effects such as floods, heat and drought. Restoring coastal wetlands can support biodiversity and reduce storm exposure.'],
    ['Disaster risk and sustainable development', 'Disasters arise when hazards interact with exposure and vulnerability. Preparedness includes drills, alerts and evacuation planning, while mitigation includes hazard-aware land use and resilient infrastructure. Sustainable development links livelihoods to long-run environmental and social costs, so a project should be evaluated across all three dimensions.']
  ],
  'g1-p-ancient': [
    ['Harappan cities and livelihoods', 'The mature Harappan period is usually dated to about 2600–1900 BCE. Sites such as Mohenjo-daro and Dholavira show planned construction, water management and skilled crafts. Standard weights, seals and materials transported between settlements point to organized exchange.'],
    ['What archaeology can establish', 'Buildings, pottery, plant remains and tools give clues about settlement and work. The Harappan script has not been securely deciphered, so seals cannot yet supply a reliable list of rulers or laws. Distinguish excavated evidence from explanations about how the cities were governed.'],
    ['Vedic settlements and institutions', 'The Rigveda preserves hymns from an early Vedic setting in which cattle and pastoral life were important, alongside cultivation. Later Vedic sources reflect eastward settlement, expanding agriculture, larger political units and more elaborate ritual traditions. Vedic texts are literary evidence and should be read alongside archaeological evidence.']
  ],
  'g1-p-religion-empires': [
    ['Religious and intellectual traditions', 'Buddhism and Jainism grew in a period of expanding towns and kingdoms in northern India. Buddhist teachings connect suffering to its causes and describe a path of ethical conduct, meditation and understanding. Jain teachings place particular emphasis on non-violence, self-restraint and release from rebirth. Upanishadic traditions explored questions about self and ultimate reality.'],
    ['The Mauryan state', 'Chandragupta Maurya established an empire in the late fourth century BCE. Ashoka’s rock and pillar edicts are primary evidence for his reign and his promotion of dhamma. His messages discuss conduct, welfare and respect across traditions, and their presence in many regions helps trace imperial communication.'],
    ['Gupta power and culture', 'Gupta rulers built power in northern India from about the fourth century CE. The Prayaga inscription records campaigns of Samudragupta, and coins help historians study rulers and royal imagery. Sanskrit literature and advances in mathematics and astronomy help explain the wider cultural significance of the period.']
  ],
  'g1-p-medieval': [
    ['Delhi Sultanate and its institutions', 'Between 1206 and 1526, five successive ruling dynasties governed from Delhi. The iqta system assigned rights to collect specified revenue to holders who performed service and helped support military and administrative organization. Alauddin Khalji’s market regulations illustrate how the state tried to supply and pay its army.'],
    ['Mughal state and agrarian administration', 'Babur’s victory at Panipat in 1526 began Mughal rule. The mansabdari system classified officers by rank and service obligations, with jagirs often used to assign revenue in lieu of cash pay. Under Akbar, revenue administration drew on measurement, assessments and regional records, with different arrangements across the empire.'],
    ['How to compare the periods', 'Compare the iqta and jagir as revenue assignments instead of treating either as an outright grant of permanent land ownership. Sultanate and Mughal rule both relied on military service and regional intermediaries, with different institutions for coordinating them. Examine chronicles and administrative texts in light of their courtly perspectives.']
  ],
  'g1-p-deccan': [
    ['Vijayanagara as a regional power', 'Vijayanagara emerged in the fourteenth century and built its capital on the Tungabhadra at Hampi. Rulers drew resources from agrarian regions and trade networks. Krishnadevaraya’s reign in the early sixteenth century is associated with military expansion and royal support for literature and architecture.'],
    ['Bahmani and successor kingdoms', 'The Bahmani kingdom became a major Deccan power in the fourteenth century. Its successors included Bijapur, Golconda and other Deccan sultanates. Competition over territories, trade networks and forts shaped the region, and the sultanates’ alliance defeated Vijayanagara’s leading forces at Talikota in 1565.'],
    ['Art, architecture and literary patronage', 'Hampi includes ceremonial platforms, temples and secular buildings that draw on multiple architectural traditions. Vijayanagara temples are associated with large halls and carved pillars, and Deccan courts also supported distinctive architecture and painting. Krishnadevaraya’s Amuktamalyada is an example of Telugu literature in the Vijayanagara court.']
  ],
  'g1-p-colonial': [
    ['From trading company to territorial rule', 'The East India Company expanded its power through war, taxation arrangements and alliances. Plassey in 1757 and Buxar in 1764 helped it gain control of revenue in Bengal. Subsidiary alliances and annexations later affected princely states, while land-revenue systems changed the relationship between cultivators, intermediaries and government.'],
    ['The revolt of 1857', 'The revolt began among soldiers and spread through many areas of northern and central India. Military grievances interacted with annexations, disruptions to landholding and local political concerns. The rebellion’s course varied by place, and in 1858 rule passed from the East India Company to the British Crown.'],
    ['Social and religious reform', 'Rammohan Roy used public argument and organization to support reform, while Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar advocated widow remarriage and education. Jyotirao and Savitribai Phule challenged caste discrimination and worked to expand schooling. Compare reformers by their methods, the groups they sought to benefit and the practices they challenged.']
  ],
  'g1-p-modern': [
    ['Organized nationalism before mass movements', 'The Indian National Congress formed in 1885, initially seeking reform through petitions, debate and advocacy. The partition of Bengal in 1905 prompted Swadeshi activity and boycotts. The Home Rule movements later pressed for self-government and helped widen participation in organized politics.'],
    ['Gandhian mobilization', 'Non-Cooperation began in 1920 and encouraged withdrawal from institutions tied to colonial rule. The Salt March of 1930 connected civil disobedience to an everyday necessity and the salt laws. In 1942 the Quit India movement demanded an end to British rule and faced wide repression.'],
    ['Multiple strands and the transfer of power', 'Revolutionary movements, labour and peasant organizing, constitutional negotiations and the Indian National Army also shaped the end of colonial rule. Indian independence in August 1947 came with Partition and large-scale displacement and violence. Build an exam timeline that links each movement’s grievance, participants, methods and effects.']
  ],
  'g1-p-ap-dynasties': [
    ['Satavahanas and Ikshvakus', 'The Satavahanas governed parts of the Deccan and their power reached the Krishna valley. Their coins and inscriptions help trace trade, rulers and patronage. The Ikshvakus followed them in parts of the lower Krishna valley, and excavations at Nagarjunakonda reveal monasteries, stupas and inscriptions.'],
    ['Eastern Chalukyas of Vengi', 'The Eastern Chalukyas established a long-lived kingdom in Vengi during the early medieval period. Their relations with the Cholas shaped politics in the eastern Deccan. The work begun by Nannaya under Eastern Chalukya patronage is a landmark in Telugu literary history.'],
    ['Kakatiyas and Vijayanagara influence', 'The Kakatiyas governed from Warangal and made irrigation tanks an important part of an agrarian landscape. The Ramappa temple is an example of Kakatiya architecture. Vijayanagara later drew Telugu-speaking territories into a wider political and cultural network, with surviving paintings and structures at Lepakshi.']
  ],
  'g1-p-ap-history': [
    ['Colonial Andhra and regional mobilization', 'Much of coastal Andhra and Rayalaseema was administered through the Madras Presidency during British rule. Revenue settlements, education and the growth of print affected rural society and public debate. Reform associations and local nationalist organizations linked regional concerns to all-India movements.'],
    ['Freedom movement in Andhra', 'Andhra activists used protests, boycotts, volunteer action and constructive work in the broader independence movement. Alluri Sitarama Raju led the Rampa rebellion of 1922–1924 in a setting shaped by forest restrictions and grievances among tribal communities. Regional struggles differed in aims and methods, so connect each event to its local circumstances.'],
    ['Making and reorganizing the state', 'The separate Andhra movement intensified following Potti Sriramulu’s death in 1952. Andhra State was formed in 1953 with Kurnool as its capital. Andhra Pradesh was created in 1956 by merging it with the Telangana region of Hyderabad State, and the 2014 Reorganisation Act provided for their separation.']
  ],
  'g1-p-ap-culture': [
    ['Architecture and the arts', 'The Buddhist reliefs from Amaravati depict scenes and symbols in carved limestone. Lepakshi’s Virabhadra temple is associated with Vijayanagara architecture and painting. The two sites differ by period, religious setting and technique, making them useful for comparative answers.'],
    ['Telugu literature and performance', 'Nannaya, Tikkana and Errana worked on the Telugu Mahabharata in different periods. Kuchipudi combines movement, music, expression and storytelling in a classical performance tradition. Gurajada Apparao used colloquial Telugu in drama to engage with social practices.'],
    ['Festivals and reform traditions', 'Ugadi marks the traditional new year in the Telugu calendar, while Sankranti is widely linked to the harvest cycle. Local celebrations and craft traditions reflect regional livelihoods and communities. Kandukuri Veeresalingam’s writing and educational work illustrates the nineteenth-century reform movement.']
  ],
  'g1-p-constitution': [
    ['Assembly and constitutional design', 'The Constituent Assembly first met in December 1946 and built the Constitution through committee work and public debate. The Drafting Committee chaired by B. R. Ambedkar prepared the draft for debate. The Constitution was adopted in November 1949 and most provisions commenced in January 1950.'],
    ['Preamble and basic structure', 'The Preamble states the aims of justice, liberty, equality and fraternity and describes India’s constitutional character. In Kesavananda Bharati in 1973, the Supreme Court held that amendments cannot damage the Constitution’s basic structure. This doctrine concerns the limits of amending power.'],
    ['Three parts with different functions', 'Fundamental Rights in Part III can be enforced through courts. Directive Principles in Part IV guide the state in governance and policymaking. Fundamental Duties in Part IVA describe responsibilities of citizens, such as protection of the natural environment. Distinguish the constitutional roles before comparing these provisions.']
  ],
  'g1-p-institutions': [
    ['Legislatures and lawmaking', 'At the Union level, Parliament consists of the President, Lok Sabha and Rajya Sabha. States have legislative assemblies and some also have legislative councils. Legislatures pass laws and budgets and use deliberations, questions and committees to scrutinize government.'],
    ['Executive and judiciary', 'At the Union level the President is the constitutional head, with executive advice provided by the Council of Ministers headed by the Prime Minister. The Governor and Chief Minister have corresponding constitutional roles in states. Courts resolve disputes, interpret laws and can review executive and legislative action for constitutional compliance.'],
    ['Major constitutional offices', 'The Election Commission supervises national and state legislative elections under Article 324. The Comptroller and Auditor General audits public finances. The Finance Commission recommends tax sharing and grants under Article 280, while public service commissions handle recruitment and advisory responsibilities under the Constitution.']
  ],
  'g1-p-federalism': [
    ['Federal division and financial relations', 'The Constitution assigns legislative subjects to Union, State and Concurrent Lists. The Union and states also share financial resources through tax devolution and grants. Coordinating institutions matter where policies cross state boundaries or are funded by one level and implemented by another.'],
    ['Emergency provisions', 'Article 352 concerns a national emergency on specified grounds, Article 356 concerns failure of constitutional machinery in a state and Article 360 concerns financial emergency. Each differs in grounds, procedure and effects. In an exam comparison, consider legislative approval and the relationship between state autonomy and Union authority.'],
    ['Local bodies and elections', 'The 73rd and 74th Amendments provide frameworks for Panchayats and municipalities. State laws determine many details of devolution, service delivery and representation. The Election Commission of India and State Election Commissions have distinct responsibilities, with the latter administering elections to local bodies.']
  ],
  'g1-p-governance': [
    ['Transparency and citizen charters', 'Transparency makes rules, criteria and decisions visible. A citizen charter typically states what a public office offers, when an applicant can expect service and how a complaint can be made. Publishing the service standard makes it possible to compare promised and actual delivery.'],
    ['Accountability in practice', 'Accountability combines answerability with consequences or correction. Records, independent audit, legislative scrutiny and grievance channels provide different ways to examine official decisions. A public agency can use complaint data to identify offices where delays recur and correct the underlying process.'],
    ['Social justice and delivery reform', 'A scheme’s formal eligibility is only one part of access. Documentation barriers, travel costs, language and online-only submissions can exclude eligible people. Assess a reform using evidence about reach, timely delivery and remedies for exclusion, alongside its stated goals.']
  ],
  'g1-p-development': [
    ['GDP, GNI and per person income', 'Gross domestic product measures the value of final production within national boundaries. Gross national income adjusts GDP for net primary income from abroad. Divide an income measure by the population to compare averages, and use constant prices to compare real growth over time.'],
    ['Planning and structural change', 'India used Five-Year Plans to coordinate public investment and development priorities after independence. Over time, agriculture, industry and services changed in their shares of output and employment. An economic reform should be assessed by the opportunities it creates and how costs are shared.'],
    ['Poverty, work and human development', 'Poverty measures may examine consumption or combine indicators of deprivation. Inequality describes how resources and opportunities are distributed across a population. Employment quality includes stability, conditions and earnings, while human-development measures incorporate education and health along with income.']
  ],
  'g1-p-sectors': [
    ['Agriculture and irrigation', 'Farming depends on access to land, rainfall or irrigation, seed, inputs, credit and markets. Canals and tanks store or convey surface water, while wells draw groundwater. Irrigation can raise output and reduce rainfall risk, although costs and groundwater depletion affect its long-term viability.'],
    ['Industry and productive infrastructure', 'Manufacturing turns materials into finished or intermediate goods and can raise productivity through scale and specialization. Transport, ports, electricity and telecommunications connect suppliers, workers and buyers. Unreliable infrastructure raises inventories, delivery times and production costs.'],
    ['Services and sectoral linkages', 'Services include logistics, retail, financial intermediation, health, education and information technology. A food processing cluster combines primary agriculture with manufacturing and services such as cold storage and market delivery. Follow one supply chain to see where jobs and value are created.']
  ],
  'g1-p-public-finance': [
    ['The public budget', 'A budget estimates government receipts and expenditures for a financial year. Taxes, fees and dividends are examples of revenue receipts. Borrowing is a capital receipt that creates a liability. Capital spending can create assets, while revenue spending supports ongoing services such as school operations.'],
    ['Tax design', 'Income tax is a direct tax because it is imposed on taxable income, while GST is an indirect tax on supplies. A progressive tax takes a larger share of income as the tax base rises. Tax design affects incentives, purchasing power and the revenue available to fund public services.'],
    ['Deficits and public debt', 'A revenue deficit occurs when revenue expenditure exceeds revenue receipts. A fiscal deficit measures total expenditure minus revenue receipts and non-debt capital receipts, indicating the borrowing requirement. Deficits are annual flows, while debt is the accumulated outstanding liability.']
  ],
  'g1-p-banking': [
    ['Money, banks and the central bank', 'Money acts as a medium of exchange, a unit of account and a store of value. Banks take deposits and lend to households and businesses, while the Reserve Bank of India carries out central banking and monetary policy functions. Changes in policy rates can influence market borrowing costs and aggregate demand.'],
    ['Inflation and financial inclusion', 'Inflation is the rate of increase in a broad measure of prices, such as a consumer price index. Inflation reduces real purchasing power if nominal incomes lag. Financial inclusion involves affordable and usable accounts, savings, payments, credit and safeguards against fraud or over-indebtedness.'],
    ['Trade and the balance of payments', 'The current account records exchanges such as goods, services and income between residents and the rest of the world. The financial account records asset and liability transactions, such as investments. Currency depreciation can make imports more costly and some exports cheaper to foreign buyers, depending on how much prices adjust.']
  ],
  'g1-p-ap-economy': [
    ['Production and regional conditions', 'Andhra Pradesh’s Krishna and Godavari deltas provide conditions for irrigated farming, while the drier parts of Rayalaseema have different water and crop requirements. Horticulture, fisheries and livestock broaden rural production and income. Industry and services grow where markets, skills, transport and power support enterprise.'],
    ['Infrastructure and regional development', 'Irrigation supports farms, while roads, railways and ports connect products to buyers. A cold-chain network may improve returns for less durable crops and seafood. Assess a proposed project by employment, market access, costs, water demand and differences between districts.'],
    ['State finance and welfare outcomes', 'The state budget separates own revenue from central transfers and borrowing. Welfare spending may improve access to healthcare, learning and secure livelihoods. Compare what was budgeted, what was spent and what changed for intended beneficiaries using state budget and survey releases.']
  ],
  'g1-p-physical': [
    ['Earth processes and landforms', 'Earth has a crust, mantle and core, and plates of the outer rigid layer move relative to one another. Tectonic processes build relief and cause earthquakes, while rivers, glaciers and wind erode and deposit material. River meanders on low gradients and deltas near mouths show different outcomes of this sediment transport.'],
    ['Weather, climate and the monsoon', 'Weather describes short-term conditions and climate describes long-run patterns. Seasonal shifts in circulation and land–sea heating help produce the South Asian monsoon. Relief affects rainfall distribution, as moist air can cool and release moisture as it rises over mountains.'],
    ['Rivers, soils and natural resources', 'A drainage basin is the land area that contributes runoff to a river and its tributaries. Soils develop through parent material, organisms, climate, relief and time, with different implications for crops and drainage. Water, soils, forests and minerals support human activity and can be degraded by damaging extraction practices.']
  ],
  'g1-p-regional': [
    ['Physical divisions and resources', 'India includes Himalayan mountains, northern alluvial plains, peninsular plateaus and coastal plains. Andhra Pradesh spans the coastal plain, the Eastern Ghats and interior uplands. The Godavari and Krishna deltas support intensive farming, while regional differences in rainfall and geology influence forests, crops and minerals.'],
    ['Forests, agriculture and mineral examples', 'Forests help regulate runoff, protect soils and support biodiversity and livelihoods. Irrigated delta areas can sustain paddy, while drier interiors can favour crops such as groundnut or millets. Geological surveys help locate mineral deposits, and transport and energy affect whether they can be used economically.'],
    ['Transport and vulnerability', 'Ports, railways and highways connect agricultural, mining and industrial centres to markets. A low-lying coastal district may be exposed to cyclone winds, storm surges and river flooding, while inadequate water storage can amplify drought inland. Evaluate both the physical hazard and the location of people, assets and services.']
  ],
  'g1-p-environment': [
    ['Ecosystems and biodiversity', 'An ecosystem includes living organisms and the nonliving conditions that support them. Energy moves from producers to consumers, while decomposers recycle nutrients. Biodiversity includes variation within species, between species and among ecosystems.'],
    ['Conservation and protected areas', 'Habitat protection helps maintain ecological relationships and viable populations. India uses national parks, wildlife sanctuaries and other conservation arrangements with different legal frameworks. Corridors, water protection and community participation can complement management inside a protected boundary.'],
    ['Pollution and climate change', 'Air pollution, contaminated water and waste affect human health and ecosystems. Greenhouse gases trap additional heat and contribute to climate change. Mitigation addresses emissions or carbon storage, while adaptation reduces vulnerability to effects such as heat, flooding and drought.']
  ],
  'g1-p-science': [
    ['Physics in everyday devices', 'Force changes an object’s motion, and pressure is force per unit area. A large blade area spreads force, while a sharp edge concentrates it. A pressure cooker raises the pressure above water so it boils at a higher temperature, and a refrigerator moves heat from a colder compartment to warmer surroundings.'],
    ['Chemistry of materials and change', 'Physical changes can alter a material’s form, while chemical changes form substances with new compositions. Rust is produced when iron reacts under suitable conditions, and paints help protect metal by reducing contact with air and moisture. pH indicates how acidic or basic a solution is.'],
    ['Biology and daily life', 'Cells are the structural units of living organisms and contain specialized components. Photosynthesis captures light energy to produce sugars, while cellular respiration releases usable energy from nutrients. Handwashing and safe drinking water reduce exposure to infectious agents through practical hygiene.']
  ],
  'g1-p-emerging-tech': [
    ['Public health and biotechnology', 'Biotechnology uses organisms, cells or biological molecules in products and processes. Diagnostic tests help detect biological markers and vaccines train immune responses. Public-health evaluation considers who can access a tool, how reliable it is and how results are acted upon.'],
    ['Space-based applications', 'Satellites provide communication, positioning and observations of Earth. Remote sensing can help track crops, flood extent and coastlines by measuring signals reflected or emitted from the surface. Field observations help validate and interpret what satellites detect.'],
    ['Digital governance and data', 'Digital systems process applications, store records and connect people to services. Their effectiveness depends on data quality, reliable access, privacy and simple appeal or correction mechanisms. Keep examples of both service improvements and barriers faced by users without connectivity.']
  ],
  'g1-p-current': [
    ['Build a three-level news record', 'Track Andhra Pradesh, India and international developments separately. For each event write the date, institution, place and why it matters. Connect a national or global item to Andhra Pradesh when there is a specific local consequence or example.'],
    ['Connect news to syllabus subjects', 'An environmental report belongs with ecology and policy, a budget change with the economy, and a successful mission with science and technology. Find the official release and separate the announced goal from expenditure, implementation and measured outcomes. When tracking a statistic, save its source, period and definition.'],
    ['Revision and verification', 'During weekly revision make one question and one short answer from each well-supported item. Lead with major governance, economy, science, environment and social developments. Review changing figures and events against their dated government, statistical or international releases close to the exam.']
  ],
  'g1-p-arithmetic': [
    ['Number systems and factors', 'Natural numbers count objects, integers include zero and negatives, and rational numbers can be written as a ratio of integers with a nonzero denominator. Prime factors make greatest common factor and least common multiple calculations easier. For 12 and 18 the highest common factor is 6 and the least common multiple is 36.'],
    ['Percentages and comparison bases', 'A percentage is a ratio out of 100. To find the percentage change, divide the change by the original value and multiply by 100. A rise from 100 to 120 is 20%, while a fall from 120 to 100 is about 16.7% because the starting values differ.'],
    ['Ratios, proportions and averages', 'A ratio of 2 to 3 divides a total into five equal parts, so a total of 50 splits into 20 and 30. Two ratios form a proportion when they are equal. Compute a combined average by dividing the combined totals by the combined counts, rather than averaging the two group averages when their sizes differ.']
  ],
  'g1-p-applied-arithmetic': [
    ['Profit, loss and discount', 'Profit equals selling price minus cost price, and profit percent uses cost price in the denominator. A discount instead uses the marked price as its base. An item marked ₹1,000 and sold at a 10% discount sells for ₹900, giving a 12.5% profit if its cost is ₹800.'],
    ['Work rates', 'Translate a worker’s completion time into a daily fraction of the job. If two people independently complete the same job in six and three days, their daily rates are one sixth and one third. Together their rate is one half per day and the job takes two days if those rates are constant.'],
    ['Speed and distance', 'Distance equals speed multiplied by time, with matching units. Average speed equals total distance divided by total travel time. Two equal-distance legs at 40 and 60 km/h have an average speed of 48 km/h, because the slower leg takes more time.']
  ],
  'g1-p-data': [
    ['Read the display before calculating', 'Identify the title, labels, scale, units, time period and data source. A table in thousands and a chart in lakhs cannot be combined until units match. Check whether a line chart covers equally spaced years and whether its vertical axis starts at zero.'],
    ['Choose the right comparison', 'An absolute change subtracts the old value from the new one and a relative change divides that result by the old value. A rise in a share from 20% to 25% is five percentage points, or a 25% increase on the original share. In a pie chart, a 90° segment represents one quarter of the total.'],
    ['Check what the data supports', 'Averages can conceal variation between places or groups. Two lines moving together suggest an association but do not establish that one variable caused the other. If population differs across places, a per-person rate may be more informative than a total.']
  ],
  'g1-p-logic': [
    ['Patterns, analogies and codes', 'Test differences, ratios and alternating rules in a sequence. The differences in 2, 5, 10, 17 are 3, 5 and 7, suggesting the next term is 26 under the next-odd-difference rule. In analogies, name the relation between the first pair before matching the second pair, and in letter codes track positions and wraparound.'],
    ['Relationships and arrangements', 'Translate family clues into a small tree and mark which relationships are stated. For seating problems, label positions and place fixed clues before relative ones. Direction terms can depend on which way a person faces, so mark orientation explicitly.'],
    ['Deductions and classification', 'Group items using a consistent property, then check whether an alternative grouping also fits. In syllogisms, test whether the conclusion must be true in every diagram consistent with the premises. From “all A are B” and “some B are C,” there is no necessary conclusion that any A are C.']
  ],
  'g1-p-decisions': [
    ['Define the problem first', 'State the decision, the objective, constraints and deadline. Distinguish established facts from assumptions and identify the missing information that could alter the outcome. A good analysis tests the causes of a delay before proposing more staff.'],
    ['Compare feasible actions', 'Lay out alternatives and evaluate expected benefits, costs, risks and impact on affected groups. If evidence is limited, consider reversible steps and plan how you will collect feedback. A short trial often tests an uncertain proposal before committing the whole budget.'],
    ['Read and communicate clearly', 'Identify the main claim, evidence and conclusion in a passage. An inference should follow from the information given without relying on a new premise. In a response, state your decision and its reason first, then explain the action, person responsible and next step.']
  ],
  'g1-p-situations': [
    ['Public service judgment', 'Administrative decisions should account for law, impartiality, proportionality and the needs of affected people. Identify the immediate risk and the applicable responsibility before judging the conduct of individuals. An urgent situation can require temporary protective action alongside formal review.'],
    ['Responding to pressure and conflicts', 'Identify actual or apparent conflicts of interest and follow the applicable disclosure and recusal rules. If a person asks for preferential processing, explain the published criteria and provide the same route available to comparable applicants. Keep records of decisions affecting eligibility or rights.'],
    ['Resolve a practical case', 'If two communities claim the same resource, verify facts, hear both parties and consider the effects on vulnerable users. Use published standards to compare solutions, record why the selected action meets those standards and set a date to review its effects. Clear communication can reduce escalation while preserving fairness.']
  ],
  'g2-s-ancient': [
    ['Harappan and Vedic societies', 'Harappan settlements show drainage, organized building, crafts and trade. Their undeciphered script leaves political arrangements uncertain. Vedic hymns and later texts describe pastoral life, expanding agriculture and social and ritual organization, and their literary setting differs from material evidence found at Harappan sites.'],
    ['Religions, states and empires', 'New towns and kingdoms formed the setting for traditions including Buddhism and Jainism. The Mauryan Empire expanded across much of the subcontinent, and Ashoka’s edicts provide evidence of his reign and moral program. Gupta rulers later built power in northern India, with inscriptions and coins recording military and political claims.'],
    ['Art and material evidence', 'Stupas, pillars, sculpture and temples can be read for materials, patrons and function. Ashokan pillar capitals and Buddhist reliefs from early historic sites are different kinds of evidence. In chronological practice, connect each example to its region and religious or political setting.']
  ],
  'g2-s-medieval': [
    ['Regional states and changing political centres', 'Different dynasties governed the subcontinent through networks around forts, agrarian districts and trading towns. The Delhi Sultanate drew revenue and military support from iqtas. In the south, Vijayanagara grew from the fourteenth century and built its capital at Hampi on the Tungabhadra.'],
    ['Mughal administration', 'Mughal rule began with Babur’s victory at Panipat in 1526 and expanded substantially under Akbar. Mansabs expressed ranks and military obligations, while jagirs often assigned rights to revenue. Study how agrarian revenues, regional allies and administration supported an extensive empire.'],
    ['Cultural developments', 'Bhakti and Sufi traditions widened the use of vernacular religious expression in different settings. Hampi illustrates the interaction of temple, courtly and urban architectural traditions. Poetry, chronicles, inscriptions, paintings and buildings preserve different perspectives on medieval culture.']
  ],
  'g2-s-modern': [
    ['Colonial expansion and social reform', 'The East India Company gained revenue and territory through war, diplomacy and annexation. Land-revenue arrangements and trade policies changed economic life, while the revolt of 1857 helped bring direct Crown rule. Reformers used education, print and organized campaigns to challenge practices affecting caste, gender and social status.'],
    ['Organizations and mass movements', 'The Indian National Congress was founded in 1885 and early leaders used petitions, debate and public campaigns. Swadeshi mobilization grew after the 1905 partition of Bengal. Gandhi’s Non-Cooperation, Civil Disobedience and Quit India movements used different methods and brought large numbers of people into public action.'],
    ['Independence and Partition', 'Constitutional negotiations, revolutionary work, labour and peasant campaigns and other movements all formed part of the independence struggle. Power was transferred in August 1947 as British India was partitioned. Link each movement’s methods and demands to a timeline rather than treating all resistance as a single campaign.']
  ],
  'g2-s-physical': [
    ['Earth, plates and landforms', 'Earth has a crust, mantle and core. Lithospheric plates move relative to each other, concentrating earthquakes and volcanic activity along many boundaries. Rivers, glaciers and wind reshape relief through weathering, erosion and deposition.'],
    ['Climate and oceans', 'Latitude, altitude and circulation influence climate. Ocean currents redistribute heat, and seasonal circulation and land–sea contrasts influence monsoon rainfall. The Himalayas, Western Ghats and local topography shape regional conditions in India.'],
    ['Rivers and resources', 'A river system drains a particular basin and moves water and sediment to lakes or the sea. Peninsular rivers such as the Godavari and Krishna are closely linked to seasonal rainfall and water management. Soil, forests, freshwater and minerals are resources whose value depends on location, access and sustainability.']
  ],
  'g2-s-economic-geography': [
    ['Population and urbanization', 'Population density describes people per unit area, and settlement patterns reflect water, terrain, livelihoods and infrastructure. Migration can follow work, education or family links. Urban growth can increase demand for housing, public transport, sanitation and employment.'],
    ['Agriculture and industry', 'Crop patterns depend on rainfall, soils, water, prices and access to inputs. Industrial locations often reflect markets, transport, reliable power, labour and access to materials. Industries that use heavy materials or perishable inputs benefit from locating near their sources when transport costs dominate.'],
    ['Transport and spatial links', 'Road and rail networks link hinterlands to towns, ports and national markets. A new link can reduce logistics costs and attract investment, while its benefits depend on which settlements it serves. Use map evidence to explain why two places differ rather than listing production figures.']
  ],
  'g2-s-ap-geography': [
    ['Land and river systems', 'Andhra Pradesh includes coastal plains, Eastern Ghats and interior uplands. The Godavari and Krishna form major deltas on the coast, and the Penna drains parts of the southern interior. River basins cross administrative boundaries, so water supply depends on upstream use as well as local rainfall.'],
    ['Climate, soils and crop patterns', 'Seasonal monsoons drive much of the annual rainfall, with strong differences across the coast and drier inland districts. Alluvial soils and irrigation support paddy in delta areas, while drier areas often support groundnut or millets. Cropping choices also reflect access to markets and water.'],
    ['Natural resources and economic use', 'Coasts, forests, fertile soils and mineral deposits support livelihoods and production. Ports and fisheries connect the coastline to trade and food supply. In coastal areas, cyclone and storm-surge risk makes settlement and infrastructure planning especially important.']
  ],
  'g2-s-society': [
    ['Diversity and institutions', 'Indian society contains differences in language, religion, region and livelihood, alongside shared legal and political institutions. Schools, families, markets and local governments organize social life in different ways. A single community label may conceal diverse experiences across locations and generations.'],
    ['Caste, gender and community', 'Caste has historically influenced social status, networks and access to occupations. Gender norms can affect mobility, schooling and the division of paid and unpaid work. Study both formal protections and the everyday circumstances that shape who can exercise a right or use a service.'],
    ['Family, change and evidence', 'Families perform roles including care, socialization and support, and their forms vary by region and economic circumstance. Migration, urbanization and education can alter household arrangements without erasing existing networks. Use survey evidence alongside examples, and distinguish group averages from claims about every member.']
  ],
  'g2-s-welfare': [
    ['Education, health and population', 'Education and health are components of human development and can improve future work and participation. Population change reflects births, deaths and migration. An indicator such as school enrolment measures a particular aspect of access and may differ from attendance or learning.'],
    ['Women, children and social inclusion', 'Nutrition, safety, schooling and healthcare affect children’s development. Barriers such as distance, household responsibilities, disability and discrimination can reduce access for eligible people. A welfare design should account for how people discover a service, qualify for it and receive help when an application fails.'],
    ['Evaluate policies in India and Andhra Pradesh', 'For each central or state welfare measure, identify its objective, beneficiaries, responsible agency and delivery channel. Compare expenditure, program reach and outcomes separately. Check the effective date and relevant government order before using eligibility details or quoted figures in an answer.']
  ],
  'g2-s-current': [
    ['Track news by geography and topic', 'Maintain separate records for Andhra Pradesh, national and international developments. For a government initiative, note the responsible institution, date, intended beneficiaries and announced change. Cross-reference state issues that connect to national policy or major international events.'],
    ['Interpret reports, indicators and technology', 'Record a statistic’s unit, reference period and issuing institution. Growth rates and levels answer different questions, and rankings depend on the report’s method and included countries or states. For a technology or environment item, write its practical application and one limitation.'],
    ['Review awards and sports with dated evidence', 'For each major award or sporting event, note the name, event, venue or year and outcome. Prefer the announcing body and official statistical releases. Build weekly recall questions rather than memorizing stray figures or headlines without context.']
  ],
  'g2-s-arithmetic': [
    ['Percentages, ratios and averages', 'A 15% increase multiplies a quantity by 1.15. If two amounts are in a ratio of 3 to 2 and sum to 40, each ratio part equals 8 and the amounts are 24 and 16. An arithmetic average is the sum divided by the count, and combined groups need weights based on their counts.'],
    ['Simple interest', 'Simple interest applies the same principal in every period. A principal of ₹2,000 at 5% per year for three years earns ₹300 in simple interest, for a total of ₹2,300. Match the time unit to the rate before calculating.'],
    ['Work, speed and distance', 'If a worker finishes one task in five days, their daily rate is one fifth of the task, and independent rates add. For motion, use distance divided by time and convert units first. A journey of 150 km in 2.5 hours has an average speed of 60 km/h.']
  ],
  'g2-s-reasoning': [
    ['Analogies, sequences and syllogisms', 'State the precise relation in an analogy and match that relation to the options. For number series, test differences or alternating patterns before choosing a rule. In syllogisms, use set diagrams to see which conclusions hold under every interpretation of the premises.'],
    ['Arrangements and directions', 'Give seats or ranks explicit positions, then assign fixed clues before applying constraints about adjacency or order. For direction problems, a sketch or grid helps separate distance travelled from displacement. Three units east followed by four units north leaves a straight-line distance of five units from the origin.'],
    ['Data sufficiency', 'The goal is to determine whether the information is enough to give a unique answer. Test each statement independently before combining them. Knowing that two positive numbers sum to ten does not give their values, but a second independent statement can fix both numbers.']
  ],
  'g2-s-data': [
    ['Read tables and graphs correctly', 'Begin with the title, axes, categories, labels, units and reference period. Tables often give exact values, while charts emphasize comparisons or trends. A truncated vertical axis can make a small numerical difference look large.'],
    ['Solve numerical comparisons', 'Use totals to find shares, earlier values as bases for percentage change, and matching numerator and denominator periods. If 72 of 240 applications are approved, the approval rate is 30%. If approvals rise to 96 while total applications rise to 320, the count rises and the approval rate stays at 30%.'],
    ['Check the limits of interpretation', 'A chart can show association without identifying causes. Use a consistent denominator when comparing districts with different populations, and consider rounding when adding reported shares. Verify that your result is plausible by estimating it before calculating precisely.']
  ]
};
